[At last, a short article for this followup!]

Well, in the AD&D scenario from which this game is evolved, we envision a
barbarian, a rogue, a priest, and a wizard entering a room.

The barbarian goes right for the rotted corpses lying about, strips weapons
and armor from them.  The rogue heads for that attractive locked chest and
checks for traps, gold, and jewels, which he'll trade in a local shop for
the stuff he needs.  The wizard beelines for the library shelves, and starts
checking out the scrolls and spell books he finds.  The priest heads toward
the family altar, but senses a coaligned quarterstaff where the others ignored
a pretty ordinary walking stick; he takes it and goes on to search for ankhs,
amulets, the occasional Book of the Dead, and whatever.

In fewer words, it is quite natural for each character class to find the
things (first) which are most important to his/her class, and to ignore
("dungeon trash") items not pertinent to his/her abilities.  So it would
not be a terrific reach to bias the types of items scattered about the
dungeon for the class of the adventurer.

This gives your wizard more magic to use.

Second, wizards are _good_ at magic, so the chances of forgetting the runes
from a spell book of magic missile should be much less for a wizard than
for a valkarie, while a healer should have little trouble retaining that
spell of extra healing in memory for many uses, although for both the concept
of mana (power) will limit spell casting in other ways.

Similarly, would a wizard botch a spell of enchant armor on that robe?  Not
likely, so he might be able to run it up to minus several without seeing it
vanish as blue vapor, and he might even sense when it was too close to risk
another spell.

Third, wizards should be able to cast spells of sharpness, keen sight, and
so on onto their little daggers, making them much less wimpy weapons, and
these spells should persist past the first use, and show on the inventory.

Similar ideas apply to other classes.

A healer might not have much strength or dexterity, but knowing where the
major ganglia and arteries lie should make the blows that do strike more
likely to be of the AD&D "critical hit" class.

Doing things should make you better at doing them, too, even things not
appropriate to your class.

Carrying around a heavy iron ball as a weapon should improve ones strength
over time, while increasing food consumption.  Reading a lot should help
wisdom or intelligence.  Using a stethescope a lot should improve ones
critical hit ratio, and eventually allow one to "remote" the stethescope,
or just recognize the signs by eye from constant association.

Popping snakes with a bullwhip should improve speed and accuracy of use,
but probably not damage done per hit.  Weilding a weapon should improve
ones chance to hit with it, as you make passes with it at the shadows while
wandering the dungeon; using it in combat should improve ones damage with
it, as one learns to find the chinks in armor.

Making all this work well might require a finer grained stat for weapons,
say in tenths or even hundredths of a "to hit" or "to damage".

Making the characters more oriented to their classes throughout the game,
rather than just at the beginning, by finding balancing "chance of finding
appropriate objects" variations and balancing advantages from class abilities
can probably be done, with lots of work and code.  The question at hand is
whether the game would still run fast enough and be small enough to be
playable on the yesterday's hardware that so many of us use.

